      <h1>Battery Regulation: Safety, Transport &amp; Environmental</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Market Intelligence · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Battery regulation sits in three layers. <strong>Product safety</strong> (IEC,
        UL) governs how a battery must behave in use; <strong>transport</strong> (UN 38.3, ADR, IATA) governs how it
        may be shipped; and <strong>environmental</strong> (the EU Battery Regulation, with its battery passport,
        carbon-footprint and recycled-content requirements) governs its whole life cycle. The EU regulation is the
        newest and most consequential shift.
      </div>

      <h2>The three layers</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Key instruments</th><th>What it governs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Product safety</strong></td><td>IEC 62133, IEC 62660, UL 1973, UL 9540A</td><td>Abuse, thermal runaway, safe operation</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Transport</strong></td><td>UN 38.3, ADR, IATA DGR</td><td>Shipping lithium cells by air, sea and road</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Environmental</strong></td><td>EU Battery Regulation (EU 2023/1542)</td><td>Carbon footprint, recycled content, battery passport, due diligence</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why the EU Battery Regulation is the big shift</h2>
      <p>
        The EU Battery Regulation (EU 2023/1542) moves batteries from a waste-era directive to a full life-cycle
        regime. Its requirements phase in over time and include a <strong>carbon-footprint declaration</strong>, minimum
        <strong>recycled-content</strong> targets, a <strong>battery passport</strong> (a digital record of a battery's
        materials, origin and performance), and supply-chain <strong>due-diligence</strong>. For a Chinese or any
        non-EU manufacturer selling into Europe, these are new compliance costs and data requirements — which is why
        the regulation is reshaping battery sourcing rather than just battery disposal.
      </p>

      <h2>Transport: why lithium is treated as dangerous goods</h2>
      <p>
        Lithium batteries are classed as dangerous goods because a damaged or shorted cell can enter
        <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lithium/thermal-runaway/">thermal runaway</a>. Shipping therefore
        requires passing <strong>UN 38.3</strong> (the eight transport-safety tests) and following mode-specific rules
        (ADR by road, IATA by air, IMDG by sea). This is why lithium battery shipping involves testing certificates
        and packaging constraints that lead-acid batteries largely avoid.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> The battery regulation that matters most for global trade is not the safety
        standard — it is the EU Battery Regulation, because it is the first to regulate the <em>whole life cycle</em>,
        and it turns data (the battery passport) into a market requirement.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Safety and transport rules are mature and mostly harmonised; the EU regulation is new,
        expanding and aimed at the supply chain itself. A manufacturer who treats compliance as a legal afterthought
        will pay for it in market access — and the battery passport is where data becomes a commercial asset.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not legal or compliance advice.</em></p>
      </div>

